import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const units=[['Position, distance, wavelength','metre','m'],['Time, period','second','s'],['Mass','kilogram','kg'],['Velocity','metres per second','m/s'],['Acceleration','metres per second squared','m/s²'],['Force','newton','N = kg·m/s²'],['Energy, work','joule','J = N·m'],['Power','watt','W = J/s'],['Frequency','hertz','Hz = 1/s'],['Voltage','volt','V'],['Electric current','ampere','A'],['Resistance','ohm','Ω = V/A'],['Spring stiffness','newtons per metre','N/m']];
---
<Layout title="Physics Units & Constants Reference" section="Formulas"><article class="article"><div class="eyebrow">Small symbols. Important differences.</div><h1>Physics units & constants</h1><p>Keep units consistent within a calculation. Most experiments use SI units; the lens explorer uses centimetres for every length, and angle inputs are labeled in degrees.</p><div class="fact-table-wrap"><table class="fact-table"><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Unit</th><th>Symbol</th></tr></thead><tbody>{units.map(row=><tr>{row.map(x=><td>{x}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div><h2>Gravity in this lab</h2><p>Earth’s gravitational acceleration is modeled as 9.81 m/s². The projectile and pendulum tools let you change it. The Moon preset uses 1.62 m/s². Practice questions explicitly state when they use the convenient approximation g = 10 m/s².</p><h2>Degrees and radians</h2><p>One full turn is 360° or 2π radians. The controls accept degrees where labeled; the models convert to radians before evaluating sine and cosine. Refractive index, friction coefficient, and magnification are dimensionless.</p><h2>Prefixes worth remembering</h2><p>1 L = 0.001 m³; 1 km = 1,000 m; 1 cm = 0.01 m; 1 mm = 0.001 m; 1 mA = 0.001 A; 1 kΩ = 1,000 Ω. Convert before substituting into a formula.</p><a href="/formulas/" class="primary-link">Find a formula ↗</a></article></Layout>
